from science_completion import q,p,m
QUESTIONS=[
q(1,'What is the dependent variable in a concentration experiment measuring gas volume per second?','Gas-production rate.','WS2.2;RPA5'),q(1,'What does a concordant titre mean?','A titre closely agreeing with other careful repeat titres.','RPA2'),q(1,'At which electrode does reduction occur?','Cathode.','RPA3'),q(1,'Which electrical supply is used for electrolysis?','DC.','RPA3'),q(1,'What is the liquid passing through filter paper called?','Filtrate.','RPA1'),q(1,'Why use a pipette filler?','To transfer measured liquid safely without mouth pipetting.','RPA2'),q(1,'What is a systematic error?','A consistent measurement bias or offset.','WS3.7'),q(1,'Which gas turns limewater milky?','Carbon dioxide.','RPA3;RPA7'),
q(2,'Distinguish a control variable from a control experiment.','- A control variable is a factor kept consistent across trials.\n- A control experiment is a comparison used to check the cause of an effect.','WS2.2'),
q(2,'Why repeat measurements and calculate a mean?','- Repeats reveal scatter and possible anomalies.\n- A mean reduces the influence of **random variation**, but does not fix a consistent bias.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'Explain the difference between accuracy and precision.','- Accuracy is closeness to the true value.\n- Precision means repeated results cluster closely; they may still all be biased.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'What should happen before deciding to exclude an anomalous result?','- Check for a procedural or measurement problem and repeat if possible.\n- Exclude only with a **justified reason**, since a real difference can also be unusual.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'Why add excess insoluble copper oxide to acid in salt preparation?','- It ensures the acid is used up.\n- Unreacted insoluble oxide can be removed by **filtration**.','RPA1'),
q(2,'Why cool a concentrated salt solution instead of evaporating it completely dry?','- Cooling reduces solubility and allows crystals to form.\n- Strong heating to dryness can cause decomposition or unwanted damage/impurities.','RPA1'),
q(2,'A burette reads 1.20 cm³ initially and 21.45 cm³ finally. Calculate titre.','- Titre = final−initial = 21.45−1.20.\n- **20.25 cm³**.','RPA2'),
q(2,'Why add acid dropwise near the titration endpoint?','- It reduces the chance of overshooting the colour-change endpoint.\n- The reacting volume can be measured more accurately.','RPA2'),
q(2,'Why does distilled water in the conical flask not change the required acid moles?','- It changes alkali concentration but not the **moles initially pipetted**.\n- The same stoichiometric acid amount is therefore still required.','RPA2'),
q(2,'Why rinse a burette with the solution it will contain?','- Residual water could dilute that solution.\n- This would create a systematic concentration error in the titration.','RPA2'),
q(2,'State the products from aqueous copper sulfate using inert electrodes.','- Cathode: **copper**.\n- Anode: **oxygen**.','RPA3'),
q(2,'Where do ions and electrons move in an electrolysis setup?','- **Ions** move through the liquid electrolyte.\n- **Electrons** move through the external wires.','RPA3'),
q(2,'Why use insulation and a lid in a temperature-change investigation?','- They reduce energy exchange with the surrounding room.\n- Measured ΔT then better reflects the reaction’s energy transfer.','RPA4'),
q(2,'Why compare temperature changes rather than final temperatures alone?','- Starting temperatures may differ.\n- Subtracting each initial value gives the actual rise/fall for that trial.','RPA4'),
q(2,'Why does a gas-collection leak reduce measured output?','- Some formed gas escapes instead of entering the collector.\n- Recorded volume/rate is therefore underestimated.','RPA5','2023 2H Q01.1: related loss mechanism'),
q(2,'Why keep total volume constant in the disappearing-cross investigation?','- It keeps solution depth comparable in the same flask.\n- Different depths could hide the cross at different amounts of sulfur, changing endpoint time.','RPA5'),
q(2,'Why must both chromatography distances be measured from the baseline?','- Both numerator and denominator must describe travel from the same starting position.\n- Measuring one from the paper edge would give a false Rf ratio.','RPA6'),
q(2,'Why use a fresh sample portion for each unknown-ion test?','- Earlier reagents could introduce interfering ions or products.\n- Separate portions reduce contamination and false identification.','RPA7'),
q(2,'Why is nitric acid used before silver nitrate in a halide test?','- Acid removes interfering carbonate.\n- Nitric acid avoids introducing chloride, which HCl would add.','RPA7'),
q(2,'Why cool an evaporating basin before weighing its residue?','- Hot apparatus can give unstable/inaccurate balance readings and damage equipment.\n- Comparable cooled readings give a more reliable residue mass.','RPA8'),
q(2,'Why does distillation require an open receiver?','- Vapour must be able to pass and condense without sealing the whole system.\n- A sealed receiver can create unsafe pressure buildup.','RPA8'),
q(3,'Describe how to separate and dry crystals after cooling a salt solution.','- Filter/separate the crystals from remaining solution.\n- Wash with a little **cold distilled water** to remove surface solution while limiting dissolution.\n- Dry between filter papers or by a suitable gentle method.','RPA1'),
q(3,'A 25.0 cm³ sodium-hydroxide sample reacts with 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ HCl. Calculate NaOH concentration.','- HCl moles = 0.100 ×0.0200 = **0.00200 mol**.\n- HCl:NaOH ratio is 1:1, so alkali moles = 0.00200.\n- Concentration = 0.00200/0.0250 = **0.0800 mol dm⁻³**.','RPA2'),
q(3,'Convert 0.0800 mol dm⁻³ NaOH into g dm⁻³, showing Mr using Na=23, O=16, H=1.','- Mr = 23+16+1 = **40**.\n- Mass concentration = molar concentration ×Mr.\n- 0.0800 ×40 = **3.20 g dm⁻³**.','RPA2'),
q(3,'Explain why temperature rise can decrease when one reactant is reduced.','- Less reactant may become limiting, so less reaction occurs.\n- Less energy is transferred to the solution.\n- For the same total mass and heat capacity, the temperature rise is smaller.','RPA4'),
q(3,'A graph tangent passes through (10 s, 12 cm³) and (50 s, 36 cm³). Calculate gas rate.','- Volume rise = **24 cm³**.\n- Time interval = **40 s**.\n- Rate = 24/40 = **0.60 cm³ s⁻¹**.','RPA5'),
q(3,'A cross disappears in 60 s, then in 30 s at another concentration. Compare reciprocal-time rates.','- 1/60 ≈ **0.0167 s⁻¹**.\n- 1/30 ≈ **0.0333 s⁻¹**.\n- The second rate proxy is **twice** the first for the same endpoint.','RPA5'),
q(3,'A chromatography spot travels 24 mm and solvent 40 mm. Calculate Rf and state a comparison condition.','- Rf = 24/40 = **0.60**.\n- It has **no unit**.\n- Compare references using the **same solvent and stationary phase/conditions**.','RPA6'),
q(3,'A 100 cm³ water sample leaves 0.20 g residue. Calculate residue concentration in g dm⁻³ and state a limit of the result.','- Volume = **0.100 dm³**.\n- Concentration = 0.20/0.100 = **2.0 g dm⁻³**.\n- The result alone does not identify harmful chemicals or microbes, so it cannot prove potable quality.','RPA8'),
q(3,'Explain how peer review and reproducibility help assess a chemical investigation.','- Reviewers check methods, analysis and whether conclusions follow evidence.\n- Independent investigators test whether similar results occur.\n- Agreement increases confidence, while errors or limitations can still remain.','WS1.6;WS3.7'),
q(4,'Describe a controlled investigation comparing temperature rise at different metal masses in a displacement reaction.','- Use measured equal solution volumes/concentrations at comparable starting temperatures in insulated cups.\n- Add a range of known metal masses with controlled piece size and mixing.\n- Record temperatures consistently to find each maximum/change, and repeat.\n- Compare ΔT, considering limiting reagent, heat loss and total solution mass; handle chemicals safely.','RPA4'),
q(4,'Explain why a correlation between acid concentration and rate needs a controlled method to support causation.','- Temperature, solid surface area or volume may also change between trials.\n- Each can affect collisions or the measured endpoint.\n- Keep these consistent while changing only concentration and repeat.\n- Then rate differences are better attributed to concentration, with uncertainty still considered.','WS2.2;WS3.6;RPA5'),
q(4,'Describe a safe aqueous electrolysis investigation with inert electrodes.','- Place the solution in a suitable vessel and separate inert electrodes.\n- Connect to low-voltage DC with named cathode− and anode+.\n- Observe/collect small product samples and use suitable gas/metal tests.\n- Keep conditions controlled, avoid short circuits and use ventilation/eye protection without inhaling gases.','RPA3'),
q(6,'Plan preparation of a pure dry copper-sulfate sample from copper oxide.','- Warm dilute sulfuric acid and add copper oxide in small portions with stirring.\n- Continue until some insoluble oxide remains, showing acid exhaustion.\n- Filter excess oxide, retaining copper-sulfate filtrate.\n- Evaporate some water gently to concentrate, then cool for crystals.\n- Separate crystals, wash with a little cold distilled water and dry appropriately.\n- Use eye protection and safe heating/acid handling, avoiding evaporation to complete dryness.','RPA1'),
q(6,'Plan a titration determining strong acid concentration from a known alkali.','- Use a rinsed pipette/filler to transfer a known alkali volume into a flask, adding suitable indicator.\n- Fill/rinse the burette with acid, fill the tip, remove funnel and read the initial meniscus at eye level.\n- Add acid while swirling, then dropwise near the endpoint over a white tile.\n- Record titre from final−initial; repeat beyond a rough run to obtain concordant readings.\n- Use known alkali concentration/volume, equation mole ratio and acid volume in dm³ to calculate concentration.\n- Average appropriate concordant readings, evaluate overshoot/dilution errors and handle corrosive solutions safely.','RPA2'),
q(6,'Plan concentration-rate investigations using both gas collection and turbidity.','- For gas collection, use known acid concentrations with the same solid mass/surface area, total volume and temperature.\n- Seal promptly and record gas volume over time, checking leaks and free syringe movement.\n- For turbidity, dilute thiosulfate at fixed total volume, add the same acid and time a common disappearing-cross endpoint.\n- Keep depth, viewing conditions and temperature consistent; calculate 1/time for comparable endpoints.\n- Repeat each condition, compare means/curves and relate trends to successful collision frequency.\n- Use appropriate acid/gas precautions, ventilation and specific limitations for each method.','RPA5'),
q(6,'Plan analysis and purification of water from different sources.','- Collect labelled representative samples and measure pH consistently with indicator/calibrated probe.\n- Evaporate known equal volumes in weighed basins, cool and weigh residue to consistent mass.\n- Subtract empty-basin mass and compare concentrations in matching units.\n- Distil another portion through a cooled condenser into an open receiver.\n- Compare original/distilled samples, considering balance resolution, contamination and substances not identified by these measurements.\n- Use safe heating/eye protection, avoid distilling dry and never infer safe drinking or taste samples solely from appearance/pH.','RPA8')]
MAPS=[
m('00-chemistry-practical-overview','Chemistry investigations and practicals',p('Fair tests and evidence','Change one variable, measure a named quantity.','Control other influential factors and repeat.','Use data, uncertainty and justified conclusions.','Peer review and replication strengthen evidence.'),p('Salt preparation','Warm acid, add excess insoluble solid.','Filter, concentrate filtrate, cool.','Separate, wash and dry crystals.','Use safe heating and chemical handling.'),p('Titration and electrolysis','Pipette known volume, burette measured titre.','Concordant titres and mole-ratio calculation.','Aqueous electrolysis: inert electrodes, DC.','Identify cathode/anode products using valid tests.'),p('Energy and rates','Insulated cup, initial and final temperatures.','Gas-volume method: measured quantity/time.','Colour/turbidity method: common endpoint, 1/time.','Both concentration-rate approaches required.'),p('Separation and tests','Chromatography: pencil line, solvent below spots.','Rf uses baseline distances to centre/front.','Ion tests: fresh samples and correct acid/reagent.','Record observation before identification.'),p('Water and evaluation','pH, residue mass and distillation.','Open receiver, separate cooling-water jacket.','Match improvement to the actual error.','No taste tests or drinkability inference from clarity.')),
m('01-investigation-skills','Planning, measurement and evidence',p('Hypotheses and variables','Prediction with chemical explanation.','Independent variable changed/selected.','Dependent measured, controls kept consistent.','Control experiment checks alternative cause.'),p('Measurements','Suitable apparatus, range and resolution.','Read scales at eye level and record units.','Calibrate/check zero.','Repeat across suitable range and intervals.'),p('Error types','Random scatter: repeats and mean.','Systematic bias: correct/recalibrate source.','Accuracy close to true, precision clustered.','Investigate anomalies before exclusion.'),p('Graphs and conclusions','Suitable bars, scatter or best-fit curve.','Label axes with quantity and unit.','Describe values, then chemical mechanism.','Correlation alone does not prove causation.'),p('Uncertainty and models','Consider instrument resolution and repeat spread.','Models simplify and have limits.','New evidence can revise explanations.','Avoid unsupported certainty from small samples.'),p('Judgement and communication','Peer review checks methods/claims.','Reproducibility compares independent work.','Risk: likelihood and consequence of harm.','Evaluate social, economic, environmental and ethical issues.')),
m('02-salts-and-titration','Salt preparation and titration',p('Soluble salt method','Warm dilute acid, add excess insoluble oxide/carbonate.','Filter excess solid, keep filtrate.','Evaporate some water then cool.','Separate, wash gently and dry crystals.'),p('Why these steps','Excess solid ensures acid used up.','Insoluble excess is removable by filtration.','Cooling gives crystals, not just concentrated liquid.','Avoid damaging sample by heating dry.'),p('Titration setup','Pipette/filler transfers known alkali.','Suitable indicator and white tile.','Burette acid, tip filled, funnel removed.','Rinse with correct solutions.'),p('Endpoint measurement','Read bottom meniscus at eye level.','Swirl, then add dropwise.','Titre = final−initial reading.','Rough run then concordant careful repeats.'),p('Concentration steps','Convert cm³ to dm³. Moles =cV.','Use balanced equation ratio.','Unknown c =moles/its volume.','g dm⁻³ =mol dm⁻³ ×Mr.'),p('Error and safety','Overshoot increases titre. Residual water dilutes burette contents.','Extra flask water does not change pipetted moles.','Eye protection, filler, safe heating.','Mean cannot correct a consistent bias.')),
m('03-electrolysis-and-temperature','Electrolysis and temperature investigations',p('Aqueous cell','Separate inert electrodes in solution.','Low-voltage DC supply.','Cathode negative, anode positive.','Ions in liquid, electrons in wires.'),p('Predict products','Less-reactive-than-H metal deposited at cathode; otherwise H₂.','Halide may form halogen at anode; otherwise O₂.','Use supplied concentration/context.','CuSO₄/inert: cathode Cu, anode O₂.'),p('Identify and control','H₂ burning splint pop; O₂ glowing splint relights.','Cl₂ damp litmus bleaches white.','Compare voltage, concentration, time and immersion.','Small quantities, ventilation, no inhalation/short circuit.'),p('Temperature setup','Insulated supported cup with suitable lid.','Measured reagents at controlled starting temperature.','Mix and record regular temperatures.','Change one chosen variable, repeat.'),p('Interpret changes','ΔT =final−initial.','Rise usually exothermic, fall endothermic.','Equal ΔT is not equal energy for different masses.','Use supplied Q=mcΔT where appropriate.'),p('Improve specifically','Insulation reduces heat exchange.','Consistent stirring and response timing.','Consider limiting reagent and incomplete reaction.','Eye protection and safe gas-producing setup.')),
m('04-concentration-and-rates','Concentration-rate investigations',p('Gas-volume method','Known concentrations, fixed total volume.','Same solid mass/surface area and temperature.','Seal promptly, measure gas at fixed times.','Repeat and compare mean curves/rates.'),p('Gas interpretation','Quantity/time gives mean rate.','Tangent gives instantaneous rate.','Leaks/start-up loss underestimate volume.','Final amount depends on limiting reagent.'),p('Turbidity method','Thiosulfate +acid forms cloudy sulfur.','Time a fixed cross disappearing.','Dilute thiosulfate but keep total volume/acid fixed.','Rate proxy 1/time for same endpoint.'),p('Endpoint validity','Same flask/depth/cross/lighting/observer.','Same temperature and mixing.','Fixed light sensor threshold improves judgement.','Do not treat proxy as molar rate without amount data.'),p('Collision explanation','Higher concentration: more particles per unit volume.','More frequent successful collisions.','Consumed reactants reduce frequency over time.','Temperature changes energy too; keep it controlled.'),p('Safe and repeatable','Eye protection and dilute/small amounts.','Ventilate sulfur-dioxide-producing reaction.','Gas syringe must move freely.','Use specific checks, not vague “be careful”.')),
m('05-analysis-and-water','Chromatography, ion tests and water',p('Chromatography sequence','Pencil baseline, small dry spots.','Solvent below starting samples.','Remove then promptly mark front.','Same solvent/paper for references.'),p('Rf and limits','Spot-centre/front distance from baseline.','Same units, no unit on Rf.','One spot may hide overlapping compounds.','Different solvent can resolve overlap.'),p('Cations','Clean loop: Li crimson, Na yellow, K lilac.','Ca orange-red, Cu green flame.','NaOH: Al/Ca/Mg white; Al dissolves in excess.','Cu blue, Fe²⁺ green, Fe³⁺ brown precipitates.'),p('Anions','Carbonate +acid: CO₂, limewater milky.','Nitric acid/AgNO₃: Cl white, Br cream, I yellow.','HCl/BaCl₂: sulfate white.','Fresh portions prevent cross-contamination.'),p('Water measurements','pH indicator or calibrated probe.','Known volume, weighed basin, evaporate/cool.','Residue mass =final basin−empty mass.','Distil water and compare samples.'),p('Safety and conclusions','Open receiver; never distil dry.','Cooling jacket water separate from vapour.','No tasting; normal pH alone not proof of potable water.','Small masses/contamination limit accuracy.'))]
